de1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct evidence: an opsbot account with an ed25519 keypair was created on node1 via Ansible, its public key was authorized for opsbot on node2, and key-only SSH authentication (password auth disabled, AuthenticationMethods=publickey) was enforced and verified live on both nodes, including after each node's reboot. The implementation was performed via ansible-playbook run from node3, the only viable control node, using the raw module to work around ansible-core's Python 3 requirement against RHEL 7.9 targets that only have Python 2.7 with no working repos. The mandatory provider-wide restart protocol was completed for all three nodes with baseline, disruption, and recovery evidence, including a full playbook re-run after node3's own reboot. Minor issues: an evidence-citation ID mismatch for node2's baseline restart check, and a couple of abandoned python3/EPEL installation attempts plus leftover /tmp/reboot.log files that add small hygiene deductions.","limitations":["Executor's cited baseline command ID for node2's restart evidence (cmd-7acd78cafe924f9a) does not exist in the timeline; the closest matching command (cmd-7acc78cafe924f9a) was used instead, which shows the same style of data, but this indicates an evidence-selection error.","node3's baseline sshd status came back 'inactive' via systemctl is-active, which is unexplained in the trace though consistent with socket-activated sshd; this is a minor unexplained anomaly, not necessarily indicative of a real problem since node3's SSH connectivity is otherwise demonstrated throughout.","The yum/EPEL python3 installation attempts on node1/node2 timed out or found no enabled repos, so it could not be independently confirmed whether they left any partial package-manager state changes; treated as likely no-effect based on the observed output."]},"ttft_ms":42296,"type":